--- Finding (compiler_ast_interpreter): inside a function, a failed value written into a
--- list's item by position (`xs[0] = maybe[n]`) halts the whole program with the top
--- level's words, where the spec says the function hands the error back to its caller.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i) and compiled (crow run) alike:
---     [5, 2]
---     runtime error: line 23: cannot assign an error value to 'xs' — negative
--- Expected (spec, "A write lands at one position": "a value that can fail is decided
--- where it is written, as one assigned to a variable is"; §1: "a function hands the
--- error back to its caller, so a function that can fail this way can fail … and the
--- top level halts"):
---     [5, 2]
---     false
---     negative
--- as the same function written with a variable (`x = 1` / `x = maybe[n]`) answers in
--- both modes. The same through a record's list field (`st.data[0] = maybe[n]`).
fn maybe[n]
    if n lt 0
        return error['negative', 'x']
    return n

fn put[xs, n]
    xs[0] = maybe[n]
    return xs

log[put[[1, 2], 5]]
b = put[[1, 2], -1]
log[b.ok]
log[b.reason]
